{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 30, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187 he was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. He became the Count of Tripoli after his father was murdered in 1152. \n*Early Life* \nRaymond III was born around 1140 he was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem his father was a member of the House of Toulouse and his mother was a member of the House of Rethel. \n*Reign* \nRaymond III became the Count of Tripoli after his father was murdered in 1152 he was only around 12 years old at the time. He was under the regency of King Baldwin III of Jerusalem until he came of age. \n*Marriage and Children* \nRaymond III married Hodierna of Jerusalem but the marriage was childless. He also had an affair with a woman named Agnes and had a daughter with her named Alice. \n*Relationship with Other Crusader States* \nRaymond III had a complex relationship with other Crusader states he was a rival of the King of Jerusalem and the Prince of Antioch. He also had a close relationship with the Byzantine Empire and received military support from them. \n*Later Life and Death* \nRaymond III was captured by Saladin in 1187 during the Battle of Hattin. He was later released and died in 1187. His death led to a succession crisis in the County of Tripoli. \n*Succession Crisis* \nAfter Raymond III's death the County of Tripoli was inherited by his godson Raymond of Antioch. However this was disputed by Bohemond III of Antioch who claimed the county for himself. The succession crisis was eventually resolved in favor of Bohemond III's son Bohemond IV of Antioch. \n*Legacy* \nRaymond III's legacy is complex he is remembered as a skilled military leader and a shrewd politician. However he is also remembered for his rivalry with other Crusader states and his failure to defend the County of Tripoli against Sala